[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Decentralized Governance, DAOs, Timelocks, and Voting Mechanisms in Web3"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Decentralized Autonomous Organizations (DAOs) represent a paradigm shift in organizational management, replacing traditional corporate hierarchies with transparent, blockchain-based smart contracts. This tutorial covers the core architectural components of Web3 governance: DAOs, voting mechanisms, proposal lifecycles, and timelocks."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Core Architecture of a DAO"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A standard DAO framework typically comprises an ERC-20 governance token, a Governor contract that manages proposals and voting logic, and a Timelock contract that executes approved transactions safely after a mandatory delay."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Implementing Governance Tokens with Votes"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To prevent flash-loan governance attacks where an attacker borrows tokens to pass a malicious proposal and returns them in the same transaction, governance tokens inherit from ERC20Votes. This extension tracks historical voting power using checkpoints."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import '@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/extensions/ERC20Votes.sol';

contract GovernanceToken is ERC20Votes {
    constructor() ERC20('GovernanceToken', 'GT') ERC20Permit('GovernanceToken') {
        _mint(msg.sender, 1000000 * 10 ** decimals());
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"3. The Voting Mechanism"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Different voting mechanisms balance participation, decentralization, and whale dominance. Common models include:"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Token-Weighted Voting: 1 token equals 1 vote. Simple, but vulnerable to whale capture."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Quadratic Voting: The cost of casting multiple votes scales quadratically, giving a stronger voice to smaller stakeholders."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Conviction Voting: Members continuously stream their voting preference toward proposals over time."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Proposal Lifecycle and Execution via Timelocks"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once a proposal passes through the Governor contract, it cannot be executed immediately. It must pass through a Timelock controller. The timelock acts as a security buffer, giving users time to exit the protocol if a malicious or contentious proposal is passed."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import '@openzeppelin/contracts/governance/TimelockController.sol';

// The timelock holds the administrative rights to protocol upgrades and treasury funds.
// Execution can only be triggered after the minimum delay has elapsed."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Designing a robust Web3 governance system requires careful consideration of tokenomics, vote delegation, quorum thresholds, and timelock delays. By combining OpenZeppelin Governor and Timelock contracts, developers can securely bootstrap decentralized decision-making for their protocols."}]
